# ClaimPulse: process mining and early-warning analytics for reimbursement claims

ClaimPulse analyses **{{ALL_claims}} real reimbursement claims** from the BPI Challenge 2020 event logs (Eindhoven University of Technology travel and expense declarations, {{first_submit_month}} to {{last_submit_month}}). The process is the same one that runs every claims operation: submit, review, approve, pay. The project builds:

- a governed DuckDB warehouse with bronze, silver and gold layers, data-quality rules with quarantine, and a provenance manifest
- operational KPIs and SLA tracking
- process mining with Celonis-style analysis in PM4Py: discovery, variants, bottlenecks and conformance
- an explainable early-warning model with a RED / AMBER / GREEN monitoring view
- a formula-driven Excel KPI pack
- a Power BI star schema with DAX measures

**Every number in this README is rendered from `outputs/audit/audit.json`**, which the pipeline writes as it runs. A test fails if any number here does not trace back to that record.

> Honest scope: these are university expense claims, not insurance or healthcare claims. The operational logic transfers: intake, multi-level review, rework loops, payment runs and SLA management. The domain does not. See [Limitations](#limitations).

## Headline findings

**1. Payment, not approval, drives cycle time.** The median claim reaches final approval in {{ALL_median_approval_days}} days, then waits {{ALL_median_payment_days}} more days to be paid. {{wait_share_post_approval}} of all waiting time between steps occurs *after* final approval. The cause is visible in the timestamps: {{payments_top2_days_share}} of {{payments_total}} payments are handled on {{payments_top_days}}, and {{payments_peak_hour_share}} at {{payments_peak_hour}}. That is a twice-weekly batch payment run, so a claim approved just after a run waits for the next one.

![Bottlenecks](outputs/figures/pm_bottlenecks.png)

**2. Rework is concentrated in one review step and slows claims down.** {{ALL_org_rejection_rate}} of claims are rejected at least once by a reviewer, and {{ALL_resubmission_rate}} are resubmitted. Administration accounts for {{top_rejecting_role_share}} of all rejections. Paid claims that were rejected along the way have a median cycle of {{median_cycle_with_rejected_any}} days, against {{median_cycle_without_rejected_any}} days for claims never rejected. Zero-amount claims are a clear quality signal: {{zero_amount_claims}} claims carry a zero amount, and {{zero_amount_problem_rate}} of them become problem claims ({{zero_amount_unpaid_rate}} are never paid).

**3. SLA performance varies widely by unit.** Against an analytical {{sla_days}}-day SLA from submission to payment, {{ALL_sla_breach_rate}} of paid claims breach. The rate is {{DD_sla_breach_rate}} for domestic, {{ID_sla_breach_rate}} for international and {{RFP_sla_breach_rate}} for requests for payment. Across the {{org_units_ge50}} unit and log combinations with at least {{org_min_claims}} claims, the breach rate runs from {{org_unit_sla_min}} to {{org_unit_sla_max}}. The worst month was {{worst_sla_month}} for {{worst_sla_month_log}}, at {{worst_sla_month_rate}}.

**4. The documented approval path is not the norm everywhere.** {{ALL_conform_rate}} of claims follow the intended approval process exactly; for international declarations the figure is {{ID_conform_rate}}. Two compliance gaps stand out:
- {{ID_r6_rate}} of international claims had the travel permit approved only *after* the trip started.
- {{ID_r7_rate}} were declared more than {{declare_window_days}} days after the trip ended.

On the review side, {{DD_r2_rate}} of paid domestic claims show no recorded administration or pre-approver check.

**5. Early warning is useful for triage, not for decisions.** A LightGBM model scores each claim at first submission. On an unseen later period it reaches ROC AUC **{{model_test_auc}}** ({{model_ci_level}} bootstrap CI {{model_test_auc_ci_low}} to {{model_test_auc_ci_high}}). For comparison, a log-type-only baseline scores {{model_test_auc_log_type_only}} and logistic regression scores {{model_test_auc_logistic}}. In the monitoring view:
- RED claims ({{rag_RED_share}} of volume) turn into problem claims {{rag_RED_problem_rate}} of the time.
- GREEN claims do so {{rag_GREEN_problem_rate}} of the time.
- RED and AMBER together capture {{rag_red_amber_capture}} of all problems.

The model's main drivers, by SHAP, are: {{shap_top1}}; {{shap_top2}}; {{shap_top3}}.

